00:00:00.880 --> 00:00:03.039 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  I'm a lawyer in legal service of  commission and responsible for the  research policy currently I'm working at  the DG environment of the European  Commission and I am a case handler  I'm a lawyer linguist in the Council on  the legal service in a Directorate known  as the quality of legislation my daily  work I assess the legislation of the  Member States where I check the  compliance of the national legislation  with the EU rules and if it's not the  case then the Commission asks the Member  States to change the legislation or  their practices lawyers here we revise  all the documents that are passed to the  commissioners for for the adoption this  is a legal revision this is revision on  the substance of the documents secondly  we represent Commission in the European  Court I have to be in regular contact  with our experts because the legislation  is is also very technical and I'm also  in contact with with different  environmental NGOs with citizens from  the Member States with representatives  from the from the industry and also  national authorities law a linguist  often confused with translators we're  not translators we're responsible as the  name of our Directorate implies for the  quality of legislation so we try and  ensure that any legislation that is  adopted by the EU is legally coherent  factually correct but also  linguistically coherent or language  versions are equally authentic so we  have to ensure that they all say exactly  the same thing  I studied law and being half English and  half Italian I studied English law and  Italian law I have a legal background so  I studied European law in Latvia I have  a bachelor's degree in science politics  and in law and master's degree in law I  first started as a trainee in the  European Commission in the in the legal  service and then I was sent to work for  the Commission as a national expert from  the from the National Authority where I  worked at the time I started my career  as a lawyer linguist in court in  Luxembourg and then opportunity arise I  was invited to work for the Commission  and I took this chance and in half a  year I started working in here in  Brussels in Commission Lingle service  you're constantly involved with the most  up-to-date legislation even before it  comes out on the news you've been  involved in to a certain extent the  negotiations for it and yes an example  was just recently working on the  accession treaty for Croatia and my  colleague that was responsible for it  then went to the the signing ceremony  metal the heads of state so there's  something quite prestigious about it I'm  working currently on the  intergovernmental treaty and so that's  yet another interesting aspect I feel in  my job that I give voice to environment  by making sure the Member States  properly apply their legislation and  their practices and if we identified  that there are some breaches then that  they're properly tackled and constant on  basis I have to find legal solutions to  a tricky situations for example  conclusion of international agreements  when the European Union concludes an  agreement with some third country first  what will we include in substance in  such an agreement to know the best serve  the you interests or some practical  considerations who will sign the  agreements  we'll be Commissioner will it be  director-general and how to be sign  shall be signed only on the last page or  does it need to be signed also on the  every page of the agreement most of the  EU institutions use English as their  working languages but then here in lingo  sirs I would say this would be  unthinkable to live also without knowing  French it happens sometimes when you get  some documents in French and then you go  to a meeting you have to express  yourself in English and then the  particularity of legal service is also  such that although we have the  Commission has three official working  languages German French and English  the European Court has only one just  French so often you have to analyze  documents which are available only in  French in the daily work I use Latvian  in English that covers a lot of Member  States because you can work with English  with most of them and the I use also  French with with my colleagues a lawyer  linguist has to speak three languages  very well to be able to get in that's  already precondition and naturally  there's always the opportunity to learn  new languages here the languages I use  essentially are English because I'm in  the English team that's the language  they use essentially in negotiations or  otherwise French the legal service is  still very much imbued with the French  culture so we do use French very often  as well  the Commission training catalog is very  big it's very diverse so basically you  can choose all sorts of training speed  the language courses or trainings for  personal development since I joined the  Commission I have followed language  classes to improve my French and I have  also followed some legal courses for  example to be acquainted with the the  new procedures of co-decision under the  Lisbon Treaty and I have also followed  some some personal development courses  like improving communication skills our  department assists us very much it  organizes a lot of training for  newcomers but also for more experienced  colleagues and I've been involved in  preparing some of those trainings and so  it's a great experience not only to  receive training but to also give  training so there's a lot of  opportunities here  I love it I wouldn't be able to adapt to  anything less than that absolutely great  perhaps I'm assisted by by my ground but  absolutely you know being in touch with  different cultures different  nationalities learning about different  traditions is is a very enriching  experience  you

